{"packet_id":"D023-01","doc_id":"D023","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California Tenant Protection Act just cause requirement","requirement":"After 12 months of continuous lawful occupancy an owner may terminate a residential tenancy only for an at-fault or no-fault just cause stated in the written notice; for a curable lease violation the owner must first allow an opportunity to cure.","key_value":"12 months' occupancy; 24 months if adult tenants are added","coverage_conditions":"Residential real property after 12 continuous months of lawful occupancy; extra rules where adult tenants are added before 24 months. Most residential rentals, subject to the listed exemptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"certificate_of_occupancy","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exempt: certificate of occupancy issued within previous 15 years; single-family owner-occupied with no more than two units or bedrooms; owner-occupied duplex; separately alienable property with non-corporate owner"},"exemptions":"Transient hotel occupancy; nonprofit hospital, religious, extended care or residential care housing; dormitories; units sharing bathroom or kitchen with the owner; single-family owner-occupied residences (up to two units or bedrooms) and mobilehomes; owner-occupied duplexes; housing with a certificate of occupancy within the previous 15 years; separately alienable property with a non-corporate owner and required notice; deed-restricted affordable housing.","penalty":"Noncompliant termination notice is void; owner liable for actual damages, attorney's fees and costs, and up to three times actual damages for willful, oppressive, fraudulent or malicious acts, plus injunctive relief.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1946.2","quoted_span":"Notwithstanding any other law, after a tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied a residential real property for 12 months, the owner of the residential real property shall not terminate a tenancy without just cause, which shall be stated in the written notice to terminate tenancy.","interaction":"Local just cause ordinances adopted on or before September 1, 2019 apply instead; more protective later ordinances also apply; a property is not subject to both.","confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D023-01","doc_id":"D023","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Relocation assistance or final-month rent waiver for no-fault just cause","requirement":"For a no-fault just cause termination, the owner must either pay relocation assistance equal to one month's rent within 15 calendar days of serving the notice or waive in writing the final month's rent, and must state that right in the notice.","key_value":"1 month's rent; within 15 calendar days","coverage_conditions":"Any tenancy for which just cause is required under the section where the owner terminates for a no-fault just cause, regardless of the tenant's income.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applies only to no-fault just cause terminations under subdivision (b)(2)"},"exemptions":"A tenant at fault for the condition triggering a government or court order to vacate is not entitled to relocation assistance.","penalty":"Failure to strictly comply renders the termination notice void; amounts provided are recoverable as damages if the tenant fails to vacate after the notice expires.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1946.2","quoted_span":"The amount of relocation assistance or rent waiver shall be equal to one month of the tenant’s rent that was in effect when the owner issued the notice to terminate the tenancy.","interaction":"Credited against any other relocation assistance required by any other law.","confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D023-01","doc_id":"D023","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Required lease notice of rent limits and just cause","requirement":"An owner must give tenants a notice, in at least 12-point type, in the rental agreement or as a signed written notice, stating that California law limits rent increases and requires a statement of cause to terminate after 12 months' occupancy.","key_value":"12-point type notice; required for tenancies from July 1, 2020","coverage_conditions":"Owners of residential property subject to the section; tenancies commenced or renewed on or after July 1, 2020 (mobilehomes July 1, 2022) need the notice in the rental agreement; existing tenancies by written notice no later than August 1, 2020.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Notice-content rule; timing turns on when the tenancy commenced or renewed"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"An owner's failure to comply with any provision of the section renders the written termination notice void.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1946.2","quoted_span":"The notification or lease provision shall be in no less than 12-point type, and shall include the following:","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
